2. How do you handle rate limiting and throttling in APIs?

| Concept           | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                 |
| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| **Rate Limiting** | A hard limit on how many API requests can be made in a **time window** (e.g., 100 requests per minute). If exceeded, requests are **rejected** (usually with HTTP 429).                |
| **Throttling**    | A **control mechanism** to **slow down** the request rate (not block), often to ensure fair use or avoid overloading the server. Requests may be **delayed** but not necessarily rejected. |
| **Implementation** | Use headers like `X-RateLimit-Limit`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining`, and `Retry-After` to inform clients about limits and when they can retry. Implement logic to handle these headers in your code. |

